message.content:
Sure!  
If you already have an array called **`myItemList`** where each element looks like  

```js
{ name: "...", status: "..." }
```

you can let the helper function handle everything for you. Here’s exactly what you’d feed into the `prioritizeAndSort` routine:

| Parameter | What goes here |
|-----------|----------------|
| `items`   | Your full array (`myItemList`) – e.g., `[ {name:"Alpha",status:"normal"}, … ]` |
| `priorityStatus` | `"urgent"` (the status you want bumped to the front) |
| `ascending` | `true` (so non‑urgent names come next in normal alphabetical order) |

### Example Call

```json
{
  "items": [
    {"name":"Zeta",      "status":"normal"},
    {"name":"Beta",      "status":"urgent"},
    {"name":"Gamma",     "status":"normal"},
    {"name":"Delta",     "status":"urgent"}
  ],
  "priorityStatus": "urgent",
  "ascending": true
}
```

When you send this payload to the `prioritizeAndSort` function, it returns a new array that starts with all “urgent” items (in whatever order they originally appeared), followed by the rest sorted alphabetically by `name`.

---

#### If you’re writing the logic yourself instead of using the helper

Here’s a quick vanilla‑JS snippet that does the same thing:

```js
const sorted = [...myItemList]
  .sort((a,b)=> {
    const aUrgent = a.status === 'urgent';
    const bUrgent = b.status === 'urgent';

    if(aUrgent && !bUrgent) return -1;
    if(!aUrgent && bUrgent) return 1;

    // both same urgency level → compare names
    return a.name.localeCompare(b.name);
  });
```

That gives you:
* All *urgent* items first (kept in original relative order)
* Followed by the remaining items sorted alphabetically.

Let me know if you’d like help wiring this up in your environment or tweaking the ordering further!
